INSERT INTO `searches_search` VALUES (1,'This paper proposes a deep learning framework for early detection of diabetic retinopathy using convolutional neural networks.','General / Best Fit','2026-03-20 20:27:24.838351',2),(3,'The exponential growth of academic literature and the proliferation of predatory publishing have significantly complicated the journal selection process for researchers. This paper presents an Intelligent Journal Recommendation & Risk-Assessment System designed to bridge the gap between manuscript content and optimal publication venues. Utilizing a hybrid retrieval architecture, the system integrates BM25-based keyword frequency with Dense Vector Embeddings to capture both lexical specificity and latent semantic intent.\n\nTo address the critical issue of research security, we introduce a multi-layered Risk-Assessment Engine that evaluates journals against established predatory indicators and historical credibility metadata. A novel feature of this framework is the Strategic Submission Planner, which categorizes recommendations into a tiered \"Plan A, B, and C\" hierarchy based on a composite score of thematic fit, SJR impact factor, and H-index. Furthermore, to mitigate common \"false positive\" matches in interdisciplinary fields (e.g., distinguishing between biological and computational neural networks), the system employs domain-specific negative filtering and contextual N-gram analysis. Experimental results on a dataset of over 2,700 high-impact journals demonstrate that the proposed framework achieves superior precision in niche identification compared to traditional keyword-based recommendation engines, providing researchers with an explainable and safety-conscious path to publication.','General / Best Fit','2026-03-20 21:05:42.596034',1),(4,'The exponential growth of academic literature and the proliferation of predatory publishing have significantly complicated the journal selection process for researchers. This paper presents an Intelligent Journal Recommendation & Risk-Assessment System designed to bridge the gap between manuscript content and optimal publication venues. Utilizing a hybrid retrieval architecture, the system integrates BM25-based keyword frequency with Dense Vector Embeddings to capture both lexical specificity and latent semantic intent.\n\nTo address the critical issue of research security, we introduce a multi-layered Risk-Assessment Engine that evaluates journals against established predatory indicators and historical credibility metadata. A novel feature of this framework is the Strategic Submission Planner, which categorizes recommendations into a tiered \"Plan A, B, and C\" hierarchy based on a composite score of thematic fit, SJR impact factor, and H-index. Furthermore, to mitigate common \"false positive\" matches in interdisciplinary fields (e.g., distinguishing between biological and computational neural networks), the system employs domain-specific negative filtering and contextual N-gram analysis. Experimental results on a dataset of over 2,700 high-impact journals demonstrate that the proposed framework achieves superior precision in niche identification compared to traditional keyword-based recommendation engines, providing researchers with an explainable and safety-conscious path to publication.','General / Best Fit','2026-03-21 05:17:01.625889',1),(5,'The exponential growth of academic literature and the proliferation of predatory publishing have significantly complicated the journal selection process for researchers. This paper presents an Intelligent Journal Recommendation & Risk-Assessment System designed to bridge the gap between manuscript content and optimal publication venues. Utilizing a hybrid retrieval architecture, the system integrates BM25-based keyword frequency with Dense Vector Embeddings to capture both lexical specificity and latent semantic intent.\n\nTo address the critical issue of research security, we introduce a multi-layered Risk-Assessm