, $BRAF$, and $HER2$ mutations, alongside microsatellite instability (MSI) status. We analyze recent clinical trial data that have established immune checkpoint inhibitors and tyrosine kinase inhibitors as first- and second-line standards for specific molecular subsets. Furthermore, we address the challenge of primary and acquired resistance, the utility of liquid biopsies in monitoring clonal evolution, and the economic implications of implementing comprehensive genomic profiling in routine clinical practice. This article provides oncologists with a structured framework for navigating the rapidly expanding therapeutic arsenal to optimize patient survival and quality of life.','General / Best Fit','2026-03-26 10:55:50.034286',3),(28,'As the global burden of cancer shifts toward younger demographics, a critical re-evaluation of screening efficacy and therapeutic equity is required. This systemic review synthesizes longitudinal data from 185 countries to provide updated estimates on incidence and mortality for 36 cancer types. We identify a significant 12% rise in early-onset colorectal cancer globally, correlating with shifts in metabolic risk factors and sedentary lifestyles.\n\nFurthermore, we evaluate the clinical integration of multi-cancer early detection (MCED) liquid biopsies compared to traditional diagnostic gold standards. Our analysis highlights a widening gap in survival outcomes between high-resource and low-resource settings, exacerbated by the high cost of personalized oncology. We propose a revised Global Clinical Framework that prioritizes risk-stratified screening and advocates for the decentralization of precision diagnostics. By aligning international policy with emerging epidemiological shifts, this report provides a strategic roadmap for clinicians and healthcare legislators to reduce the global cancer burden by 2030.','General / Best Fit','2026-03-26 10:57:08.528515',3),(29,'As the global burden of cancer shifts toward younger demographics, a critical re-evaluation of screening efficacy and therapeutic equity is required. This systemic review synthesizes longitudinal data from 185 countries to provide updated estimates on incidence and mortality for 36 cancer types. We identify a significant 12% rise in early-onset colorectal cancer globally, correlating with shifts in metabolic risk factors and sedentary lifestyles.\n\nFurthermore, we evaluate the clinical integration of multi-cancer early detection (MCED) liquid biopsies compared to traditional diagnostic gold standards. Our analysis highlights a widening gap in survival outcomes between high-resource and low-resource settings, exacerbated by the high cost of personalized oncology. We propose a revised Global Clinical Framework that prioritizes risk-stratified screening and advocates for the decentralization of precision diagnostics. By aligning international policy with emerging epidemiological shifts, this report provides a strategic roadmap for clinicians and healthcare legislators to reduce the global cancer burden by 2030.','Cross-disciplinary Science','2026-03-26 11:00:03.493665',3),(30,'As the global burden of cancer shifts toward younger demographics, a critical re-evaluation of screening efficacy and therapeutic equity is required. This systemic review synthesizes longitudinal data from 185 countries to provide updated estimates on incidence and mortality for 36 cancer types. We identify a significant 12% rise in early-onset colorectal cancer globally, correlating with shifts in metabolic risk factors and sedentary lifestyles.\n\nFurthermore, we evaluate the clinical integration of multi-cancer early detection (MCED) liquid biopsies compared to traditional diagnostic gold standards. Our analysis highlights a widening gap in survival outcomes between high-resource and low-resource settings, exacerbated by the high cost of personalized oncology. We propose a revised Global Clinical Framework that prioritizes risk-stratified screening and advocates for the decentralization of precision diagnostics. By aligning international policy with emerging epidemiological shifts, this report provides a strategic roadmap for clinicians and healthcare legislators to reduce the global cancer burden by 2030.','Clinical Impact','2026-03-26 11:01:13.964732',3),(31,'As the global burden of cancer shifts toward younger demographics, a critical re-evaluation of screening efficacy and therapeutic equity is required. This systemic review synthesizes longitudinal data from 185 countries to provide updated estimates on incidence and mortality for 36 cancer types. We identify a significant 12% rise in early-onset colorectal cancer globally, correlating with shifts in metabolic risk factors and sedentary lifestyles.\n\nFurthermore, we evaluate the clinical integration of multi-cancer early detection (MCED) liquid biopsies compared to traditional diagnostic gold standards. Our analysis highlights a widening gap in survival outcomes between high-resource and low-resource settings, exacerbated by the high cost of personalized oncology. We propose a revised Global Clinical Framework that prioritizes risk-stratified screening and advocates for the decentralization of precision diagnostics. By aligning international policy with emerging epidemiological shifts, this report provides a strategic roadmap for clinicians and healthcare legislators to reduce the global cancer burden by 2030','General / Best Fit','2026-03-27 15:37:50.024880',1),(32,'As the global burden of cancer shifts toward younger demographics, a critical re-evaluation of screening efficacy and therapeutic equity is required. This systemic review synthesizes longitudinal data from 185 countries to provide updated estimates on incidence and mortality for 36 cancer types. We identify a significant 12% rise in early-onset colorectal cancer globally, correlating with shifts in metabolic risk factors and sedentary lifestyles.\n\nFurthermore, we evaluate the clinical integration of multi-cancer early detection (MCED) liquid biopsies compared to traditional diagnostic gold standards. Our analysis highlights a widening gap in survival outcomes between high-resource and low-resource settings, exacerbated by the high cost of personalized oncology. We propose a revised Global Clinical Framework that prioritizes risk-stratified screening and advocates for the decentralization of precision diagnostics. By aligning international policy with emerging epidemiological shifts, this report provides a strategic roadmap for clinicians and healthcare legislators to reduce the global cancer burden by 2030','General / Best Fit','2026-03-27 16:12:56.999440',1),(33,'The earliest morphologically identifiable dogs are from Europe and date to at least 14,000 years ago1,2,3,4,5, although early remains are also found in other regions. The origin of early dogs in Europe, and their relationships to other dogs, has remained elusive in the absence of genome-wide data. Similarly, although dogs were the only domestic animal to predate agriculture, little is known about how the arrival of Neolithic farmers from Southwest Asia affected the dogs living with European Mesolithic hunter-gatherers. Here we analysed 216 canid remains, including 181 from Palaeolithic and Mesolithic Europe. We developed a genome-wide capture approach that enriched endogenous DNA by 10–100-fold and could distinguish dog from wolf ancestry for 141 of 216 remains. The oldest dog data that we recovered are from a 14,200-year-old dog from the Kesslerloch site in Switzerland, and we find that it shares ancestry with later worldwide dogs—inconsistent with the hypothesis that European Upper Palaeolithic dogs derived wholly from a separate domestication process. The Kesslerloch dog already displays more affinity to Mesolithic, Neolithic and present-day European dogs than to Asian dogs, demonstrating that dog genetic diversification had started well before 14,200 years ago. We find a Neolithic influx of Southwest Asian ancestry into Europe, but this seems to have been of smaller magnitude than in humans, suggesting that Mesolithic dogs contributed substantially to Neolithic, and, ultimately, probably also modern, European dogs.','General / Best Fit','2026-03-27 16:25:24.489516',1),(34,'The earliest morphologically identifiable dogs are from Europe and date to at least 14,000 years ago1,2,3,4,5, although early remains are also found in other regions. The origin of early dogs in Europe, and their relationships to other dogs, has remained elusive in the absence of genome-wide data. Similarly, although dogs were the only domestic animal to predate agriculture, little is known about how the arrival of Neolithic farmers from Southwest Asia affected the dogs living with European Mesolithic hunter-gatherers. Here we analysed 216 canid remains, including 181 from Palaeolithic and Mesolithic Europe. We developed a genome-wide capture approach that enriched endogenous DNA by 10–100-fold and could distinguish dog from wolf ancestry for 141 of 216 remains. The oldest dog data that we recovered are from a 14,200-year-old dog from the Kesslerloch site in Switzerland, and we find that it shares ancestry with later worldwide dogs—inconsistent with the hypothesis that European Upper Palaeolithic dogs derived wholly from a separate domestication process. The Kesslerloch dog already displays more affinity to Mesolithic, Neolithic and present-day European dogs than to Asian dogs, demonstrating that dog genetic diversification had started well before 14,200 years ago. We find a Neolithic influx of Southwest Asian ancestry into Europe, but this seems to have been of smaller magnitude than in humans, suggesting that Mesolithic dogs contributed substantially to Neolithic, and, ultimately, probably also modern, European dogs.','General / Best Fit','2026-03-29 17:57:54.539252',1),(35,'The earliest morphologically identifiable dogs are from Europe and date to at least 14,000 years ago1,2,3,4,5, alth