ng and parole decisions across US jurisdictions. Analysis of COMPAS, PSA, and Arnold Foundation tools reveals systematic disparate impact across racial and socioeconomic groups that persists under individual fairness constraints. We propose a multi-objective optimization framework for simultaneously satisfying legal disparate impact thresholds and predictive accuracy requirements. Constitutional implications under the Equal Protection Clause are analyzed through three circuit court decisions.','General / Best Fit','2026-04-08 17:17:23.964011',1),(145,'According to the WHO, diabetes mellitus is a long-term condition marked by high blood sugar levels. The consequences might be far-reaching. According to current increases in mortality, diabetes has risen to number 10 among the leading causes of mortality worldwide. When used to predict diabetes using unbalanced datasets from testing, machine learning (ML) classifiers and established approaches for encoding categorical data have exhibited a broad variety of surprising outcomes. Early studies also made use of an artificial neural network to extract features without obtaining a grasp of the sequence information.This study offers a deep learning-based decision support system (DSS), utilizing bidirectional long/short-term memory (BiLSTM), to accurately predict diabetic illness from patient data. In order to predict diabetes, the BiLSTM hybrid model was used after balancing the data set.','General / Best Fit','2026-04-08 17:18:30.257737',1),(146,'According to the WHO, diabetes mellitus is a long-term condition marked by high blood sugar levels. The consequences might be far-reaching. According to current increases in mortality, diabetes has risen to number 10 among the leading causes of mortality worldwide. When used to predict diabetes using unbalanced datasets from testing, machine learning (ML) classifiers and established approaches for encoding categorical data have exhibited a broad variety of surprising outcomes. Early studies also made use of an artificial neural network to extract features without obtaining a grasp of the sequence information.This study offers a deep learning-based decision support system (DSS), utilizing bidirectional long/short-term memory (BiLSTM), to accurately predict diabetic illness from patient data. In order to predict diabetes, the BiLSTM hybrid model was used after balancing the data set.','Clinical Impact','2026-04-08 17:20:53.531220',1),(147,'Breast cancer is the most common malignant tumor among women worldwide and remains one of the leading causes of death among women. Its incidence and mortality rates are continuously rising. In recent years, with the rapid advancement of deep learning (DL) technology, DL has demonstrated significant potential in breast cancer diagnosis, prognosis evaluation, and treatment response prediction. This paper reviews relevant research progress and applies DL models to image enhancement, segmentation, and classification based on large-scale datasets from TCGA and multiple centers. We employed foundational models such as ResNet50, Transformer, and Hover-net to investigate the performance of DL models in breast cancer diagnosis, treatment, and prognosis prediction. The results indicate that DL techniques have significantly improved diagnostic accuracy and efficiency, particularly in predicting breast cancer metastasis and clinical prognosis. Furthermore, the study emphasizes the crucial role of robust databases in developing highly generalizable models. Future research will focus on addressing challenges related to data management, model interpretability, and regulatory compliance, ultimately aiming to provide more precise clinical treatment and prognostic evaluation programs for breast cancer patients.','General / Best Fit','2026-04-08 17:24:15.323455',1),(148,'Depression is a prevalent global mental health disorder with substantial individual and societal impact. Natural language processing (NLP), a branch of artificial intelligence, offers the potential for improving depression screening by extracting meaningful information from textual data, but there are challenges and ethical considerations.This literature review aims to explore existing NLP methods for detecting depression, discuss successes and limitations, address ethical concerns, and highlight potential biases.A literature search was conducted using Semantic Scholar, PubMed, and Google Scholar to identify studies on depression screening using NLP. Keywords included “depression screening,” “depression detection,” and “natural language processing.” Studies were included if they discussed the application of NLP techniques for depression screening or detection. Studies were screened and selected for relevance, with data extracted and synthesized to identify common themes and gaps in the literature.','General / Best Fit','2026-04-08 17:27:00.782547',1),(149,'Depression is a prevalent global mental health disorder with substantial individual and societal impact. Natural language processing (NLP), a branch of artificial intelligence, offers the potential for improving depression screening by extracting meaningful information from textual data, but there are challenges and ethical considerations.This literature review aims to explore existing NLP methods for detecting depression, discuss successes and limitations, address ethical concerns, and highlight potential biases.A literature search was conducted using Semantic Scholar, PubMed, and Google Scholar to identify studies on depression screening using NLP. Keywords included “depression screening,” “depression detection,” and “natural language processing.” Studies were included if they discussed the application of NLP techniques for depression screening or detection. Studies were screened and selected for relevance, with data extracted and synthesized to identify common themes and gaps in the literature.','General / Best Fit','2026-04-08 17:27:34.773385',1),(150,'Depression is a prevalent global mental health disorder with substantial individual and societal impact. Natural language processing (NLP), a branch of artificial intelligence, offers the potential for improving depression screening by extracting meaningful information from textual data, but there are challenges and ethical considerations.This literature review aims to explore existing NLP methods for detecting depression, discuss successes and limitations, address ethical concerns, and highlight potential biases.A literature search was conducted using Semantic Scholar, PubMed, and Google Scholar to identify studies on depression screening using NLP. Keywords included “depression screening,” “depression detection,” and “natural language processing.” Studies were included if they discussed the application of NLP techniques for depression screening or detection. Studies were screened and selected for relevance, with data extracted and synthesized to identify common themes and gaps in the literature.','General / Best Fit','2026-04-09 07:13:17.258855',1),(151,'As the global burden of cancer shifts toward younger demographics, a critical re-evaluation of screening efficacy and therapeutic equity is required. This systemic review synthesizes longitudinal data from 185 countries to provide updated estimates on incidence and mortality for 36 cancer types. We identify a significant 12% rise in early-onset colorectal cancer globally, correlating with shifts in metabolic risk factors and sedentary lifestyles.\n\nFurthermore, we evaluate the clinical integration of multi-cancer early detection (MCED) liquid biopsies compared to traditional diagnostic gold standards. Our analysis highlights a widening gap in survival outcomes between high-resource and low-resource settings, exacerbated by the high cost of personalized oncology. We propose a revised Global Clinical Framework that prioritizes risk-stratified screening and advocates for the decentralization of precision diagnostics. By aligning international policy with emerging epidemiological shifts, this report provides a strategic roadmap for clinicians and healthcare legislators to reduce the global cancer burden by 2030','General / Best Fit','2026-04-09 07:16:20.767204',1),(152,'As the global burden of cancer shifts toward younger demographics, a critical re-evaluation of screening efficacy and therapeutic equity is required. This systemic review synthesizes longitudinal data from 185 countries to provide updated estimates on incidence and mortality for 36 cancer types. We identify a significant 12% rise in early-onset colorectal cancer globally, correlating with shifts in metabolic risk factors and sedentary lifestyles.\n\nFurthermore, we evaluate the clinical integration of multi-cancer early detection (MCED) liquid biopsies compared to traditional diagnostic gold standards. Our analysis highlights a widening gap in survival outcomes between high-resource and low-resource settings, exacerbated by the high cost of personalized oncology. We propose a revised Global Clinical Framework that prioritizes risk-stratified screening and advocates for the decentralization of precision diagnostics. By aligning international policy with emerging epidemiological shifts, this report provides a strategic roadmap for clinicians and healthcare legislators to reduce the global cancer burden by 2030','General / Best Fit','2026-04-09 07:20:30.111931',1);
